Project: Django. Upgrade: 3.2 -> 4.2. Release of these items: 4.2.
Source: Django 4.2 release notes.

Label every item:
- risk = the change could break an application or deployment, require migration work, or create operational risk for SOME user upgrading across this release (removed or renamed APIs/settings/flags, changed defaults, behavior changes, raised minimum versions of runtimes/databases/dependencies, data or schema migration, required config changes, deprecated-and-removed features).
- context = accurate and useful but needs no user action at this release: explicitly "no user action required", a deprecation that still works (warning only), a purely additive new feature or option, documentation pointers.
- noise = credits, install/download commands, pure bug fixes that cannot change correct behavior, marketing text, headings with no content.

Return one label per item_id with a one-sentence reason grounded in the item text.

Items (JSON lines):
{"item_id": "d0ed878046d6", "section": "Django 4.2 release notes", "text": "*April 3, 2023* Welcome to Django 4.2! These release notes cover the new features, as well as some backwards incompatible changes you'll want to be aware of when upgrading from Django 4.1 or earlier. We've begun the deprecation process for some features. See the /howto/upgrade-version guide if you're updating an existing project. Django 4.2 is designated as a long-term support release. It will receive security updates for at least three years after its release. Support for the previous LTS, Django 3.2, will end in April 2024."}
{"item_id": "17eb8e735cf2", "section": "Django 4.2 release notes / Python compatibility", "text": "Django 4.2 supports Python 3.8, 3.9, 3.10, 3.11, and 3.12 (as of 4.2.8). We highly recommend and only officially support the latest release of each series."}
{"item_id": "1a184ee069dc", "section": "Django 4.2 release notes / What's new in Django 4.2 / Psycopg 3 support", "text": "Django now supports psycopg version 3.1.8 or higher. To update your code, install the psycopg library, you don't need to change the ENGINE as django.db.backends.postgresql supports both libraries. Support for psycopg2 is likely to be deprecated and removed at some point in the future. Be aware that psycopg 3 introduces some breaking changes over psycopg2. As a consequence, you may need to make some changes to account for differences from psycopg2."}
{"item_id": "2ec0a1679b97", "section": "Django 4.2 release notes / What's new in Django 4.2 / Comments on columns and tables", "text": "The new Field.db_comment and Meta.db_table_comment options allow creating comments on columns and tables, respectively. For example: from django.db import models class Question(models.Model): text = models.TextField(db_comment=\"Poll question\") pub_date = models.DateTimeField( db_comment=\"Date and time when the question was published\", ) class Meta: db_table_comment = \"Poll questions\" class Answer(models.Model): question = models.ForeignKey( Question, on_delete=models.CASCADE, db_comment=\"Reference to a question\", ) answer = models.TextField(db_comment=\"Question answer\") class Meta: db_table_comment = \"Question answers\""}
{"item_id": "d80f8703e138", "section": "Django 4.2 release notes / What's new in Django 4.2 / Comments on columns and tables", "text": "Also, the new AlterModelTableComment operation allows changing table comments defined in the Meta.db_table_comment."}
{"item_id": "33f5df64d892", "section": "Django 4.2 release notes / What's new in Django 4.2 / Mitigation for the BREACH attack", "text": "GZipMiddleware now includes a mitigation for the BREACH attack. It will add up to 100 random bytes to gzip responses to make BREACH attacks harder. Read more about the mitigation technique in the Heal The Breach (HTB) paper."}
{"item_id": "6fdd1415c046", "section": "Django 4.2 release notes / What's new in Django 4.2 / In-memory file storage", "text": "The new django.core.files.storage.InMemoryStorage class provides a non-persistent storage useful for speeding up tests by avoiding disk access."}
{"item_id": "f9e770ea346c", "section": "Django 4.2 release notes / What's new in Django 4.2 / Custom file storages", "text": "The new STORAGES setting allows configuring multiple custom file storage backends. It also controls storage engines for managing files (the \"default\" key) and static files (the \"staticfiles\" key). The old DEFAULT_FILE_STORAGE and STATICFILES_STORAGE settings are deprecated as of this release."}
{"item_id": "81ab0885142a", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.admin", "text": "The light or dark color theme of the admin can now be toggled in the UI, as well as being set to follow the system setting."}
{"item_id": "f9c4f11d8139", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.admin", "text": "The admin's font stack now prefers system UI fonts and no longer requires downloading fonts. Additionally, CSS variables are available to more easily override the default font families."}
{"item_id": "eac45dc3792c", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.admin", "text": "The admin/delete_confirmation.html template now has some additional blocks and scripting hooks to ease customization."}
{"item_id": "0bd5044ce5f2", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.admin", "text": "The chosen options of filter_horizontal and filter_vertical widgets are now filterable."}
{"item_id": "efc985f3d8e5", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.admin", "text": "The admin/base.html template now has a new block nav-breadcrumbs which contains the navigation landmark and the breadcrumbs block."}
{"item_id": "adb6b74c90c0", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.admin", "text": "ModelAdmin.list_editable now uses atomic transactions when making edits."}
{"item_id": "a1bed74e7e0b", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.admin", "text": "jQuery is upgraded from version 3.6.0 to 3.6.4."}
{"item_id": "718a4dad75ad", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.auth", "text": "The default iteration count for the PBKDF2 password hasher is increased from 390,000 to 600,000."}
{"item_id": "066cdf6e86f1", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.auth", "text": "UserCreationForm now saves many-to-many form fields for a custom user model."}
{"item_id": "9661653ab636", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.auth", "text": "The new BaseUserCreationForm is now the recommended base class for customizing the user creation form."}
{"item_id": "bd2b084c07da", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.gis", "text": "The GeoJSON serializer now outputs the id key for serialized features, which defaults to the primary key of objects."}
{"item_id": "22c827a1aa4d", "section": "What's new in Django 4.2 / Minor features / django.contrib.gis", "text": "The GDALRaster class now supports pathlib.Path."}
